From dd5756d586dd80acf2552170c52df6ca47cee05f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dgabehar <dgabehar@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 22:24:33 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] common : split-balance: calibrate a layer's decode ops long
 enough to pay the real per-submit cost, not just its own norms/RoPE nodes

Round 8 QA (docs/qa/layer-calibration-dawn-qa-round8-2026-09-25.md) found
b4a0f6c85/9ecf429da did not fix the dense/hybrid tg overestimate on
gabesrv01 (gfx90c, GGML_VK_MAX_NODES_PER_SUBMIT=1): K2-Horizon-7B still
predicted 7.3 t/s against a measured 0.91 (+702%), Qwen3.5-4B 11.1 vs
1.98 (+461%). gpt-oss-20b (MoE) stayed accurate, but only because auto
put 0 of its layers on gabesrv01 in the first place.

Root cause: b4a0f6c85 only lengthened the untimed-op probe (t_op_tg,
the chain of scale ops pricing a layer's norms/RoPE/elementwise nodes
per node) to 256 nodes so it would reach the same submit-queue depth a
real decode graph runs at. It never touched the OTHER graph
calibrate_device times per layer -- the weight matmuls, attention and
GDN ops (calib_layer_graphs::decode) -- which stayed a short,
per-layer-kind graph (as few as 8-10 nodes). Under a backend that
submits one command buffer per node, that graph never reaches the
submit-queue depth a real many-layer decode graph reaches (amdgpu's
32-job sched_jobs ring on gfx90c), so it measured the cheap,
unsaturated per-submit cost for its own weight matmuls, then that
per-layer time was multiplied by n_layers to predict the whole model --
the majority of the model's real per-token cost (dozens of layers x
node count, once the ring backs up and vkQueueSubmit blocks) was never
in the measurement at all. gpt-oss-20b's calibration graph has the same
shape, but since auto placed 0 layers on the affected device, the bug
never had anything to manifest on there.

Fix: repeat a layer's decode ops (weight matmuls, attention, GDN) in
calibrate_device until the graph reaches the same length as the
untimed-op probe (n_chain, now shared between the two), then divide the
measured time by the repeat count to get a per-layer average -- same
technique b4a0f6c85 already used for the untimed ops, applied to the
ops that were still measured on a too-short graph. Prefill is not
repeated: each of its nodes already processes a whole ubatch, so the
same fixed per-submit cost is a much smaller share of it (matches the
QA data: pp read only 20-40% high, not 6-8x). The "layer's other ops"
node-count accounting (nodes_tg_layer/nodes_pp_layer minus what was
timed) now reads the single-pass op counts directly instead of counting
nodes in the (now-repeated) graphs, avoiding double-counting.

Local verification (RTX 3080 Ti and Intel UHD 770 iGPU with
GGML_VK_MAX_NODES_PER_SUBMIT=1, K2-Horizon-7B split across the iGPU +
a local CPU RPC worker): neither reproduces the amdgpu-specific
submit-queue stall this fix targets -- Intel's driver doesn't share
amdgpu's 32-job ring, and ggml-vulkan.cpp's own small-submission
handling (flops_per_submit/AMD_GCN checks in ggml_vk_graph_compute) is
gated on VK_VENDOR_ID_AMD. Predicted vs measured stayed close (auto
split 15/21 layers: pred tg 4.7/4.9 t/s before/after this fix, measured
6.5 t/s both times; forced all-36-layers-on-iGPU: measured 3.72 t/s) --
i.e. no regression on hardware the bug doesn't affect, but also nothing
that proves the fix on the hardware where the bug lives. Cluster
verification against gabesrv01+07 (the reproducing setup) follows in
this branch's next commit / this PR's description.

New test: test_decode_replication_normalizes_rate (tests/test-split-
balance.cpp), CPU-backend, checks that splitting a layer's decode bytes
across 1 weight vs 8 weights (very different repeat counts) calibrates
to close to the same rate -- the repeat-and-normalize math must not
bias the result by how many ops a layer happens to have.

ctest -L main 60/60, ctest -R split-balance 1/1.

Assisted-by: Claude Sonnet 5
---
 common/split-balance.cpp     | 133 +++++++++++++++++++++++------------
 tests/test-split-balance.cpp |  67 ++++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 155 insertions(+), 45 deletions(-)

diff --git a/common/split-balance.cpp b/common/split-balance.cpp
